Yes. “Inner speech” is only one channel in a much larger self-to-self communication stack.

You can think of the mind-body system as communicating through several representational formats at once: verbal language, visual imagery, motor rehearsal, posture, breathing, autonomic arousal, interoceptive sensations, affect, attentional bias, habit, and narrative simulation. Hormones and neuromodulators also change the system’s operating state, though those are usually slower regulatory signals rather than deliberate sentences.

A useful distinction is between symbolic communication and state communication. Symbolic communication says, “I am in danger.” State communication simply puts the organism into the configuration associated with danger: tightened muscles, narrowed attention, altered breathing, faster heart rate, changed action readiness. The receiver doesn’t need to translate a sentence because the meaning is already embodied in the state.

That gives you something like:

words → propositions

images → spatial/scene models

body state → action tendencies

emotion → value / priority

instinct → compressed policy

story → simulated trajectory

pure meaning → relational structure before wording

And the “pure meaning” part is especially interesting. People often know what they mean before they know how to say it. Language can be understood as a serialization layer: a high-dimensional internal state gets squeezed into a linear sequence of words. The thought is not necessarily made of sentences beforehand.

Stories are even more powerful because they are executable models. Instead of telling yourself ten explicit rules, you can generate a scene containing an agent, goal, obstacle, stakes, and outcome. Your predictive machinery can then run the scene and extract the lesson implicitly.

So:

story = compressed behavioral simulator

You can tell yourself “be courageous,” or you can imagine yourself walking into the room, feeling the resistance, acting anyway, and seeing what follows. The second representation recruits much more of the system.

There is also a recursive version. You can deliberately alter one channel to send information into another:

posture → affect → interpretation

breathing → autonomic state → attention

music → affect → imagery → narrative

imagined scene → physiological response → motivation

movement → proprioception → identity/state inference

The organism is effectively writing messages into its own state space and then reading them back.

That connects directly to your uber-sense idea. An “uber sense” need not only be a way of sensing the external world. There can also be uber-interoception: becoming sensitive to normally implicit internal signals and learning what they predict.

Eventually the distinction becomes less “I talk to myself” and more:

one subsystem perturbs the shared state so another subsystem can understand it.

Language is just one protocol among many.